verdict.fix_notes:
Wrap the feed->dispatch region of _SpaceTap in Critical with save/restore, mirroring _OnPrefixChar:

	prev := Critical("On")
	try {
		TextPressKey("Space", "")
		HSEMatch := HSE_FeedChar(" ")
		if (HSEMatch != "") { ... HSE_DispatchMatch(...) ... }
		else { _ResetPrefixBuffer(); ... }
	} finally {
		Critical(prev)
	}

Notes:
- Save/restore (not bare Critical("On")/Critical("Off")) so it nests safely if a future caller is already Critical, matching HSE_DispatchMatch's _AtCrit pattern.
- It is SAFE to hold across this region: HSE_FeedChar and the atomic branch do no Sleep. The ONE Sleep is the Notepad clipboard branch, and HSE_DispatchMatch ALREADY drops Critical itself there (engine 1307 Critical("Off") ... 1312 restore), so nesting under the new outer Critical is fine — it will be re-entered correctly.
- Include TextPressKey("Space") INSIDE the Critical region. The space here is the end-char that makes the trigger match (e.g. ia+space -> IA); it must be emitted/fed for the match to occur. The finding's "emit the space AFTER the expansion decision" suggestion is unnecessary and risks breaking end-char matching — Critical alone closes the splice window without reordering.
- _SpaceTapHoldLayer / hold variants do not dispatch hotstrings, so they need no change. Only _SpaceTap (and by extension _SpaceDispatch when it routes to _SpaceTap) needs the wrap.

The proposed fix is correct in spirit but the rationale should be corrected. Keep two changes: (1) bound the wait so a genuinely-lost up-event cannot latch LShift forever — `KeyWait("SC038","T5")` (drop the redundant "U"; the no-option default already waits for up). (2) guarantee the paired release with try/finally:

  TextPressKey("LShift","Down")
  try {
      KeyWait("SC038","T5")
  } finally {
      TextPressKey("LShift","Up")
  }

This makes the release run on the normal return, on timeout, and on any (currently non-existent but future) throw. Do NOT motivate this fix with the Suspend scenario — that is refuted by AHK thread semantics (an interrupted KeyWait thread resumes and still runs the Up). The real justification is the unbounded KeyWait + held synthetic modifier on the hot one_shot_shift tap path, plus consistency with space.ahk's `U T2` and rctrl's `U`.

Separately worth noting (out of scope for this finding): the global error handler releases stuck modifiers using GetKeyState(...,"P") (physical), so it cannot recover a synthetically-held modifier; the try/finally above is what actually closes that gap for this path.

rctrl.ahk 7.3 already arms LShift only after the tap/hold split and waits on the same physical key — applying the same try/finally + timeout there is a cheap, consistent hardening, not a fix for an equivalent bug.

allFindings.description:
The failure-handling block reads:
  if (!HBmp or !MemDC) {
      if HBmp DllCall("Gdi32\DeleteObject", "Ptr", HBmp)
          if MemDC DllCall("Gdi32\DeleteDC", "Ptr", MemDC)
              return
  }
The DeleteDC and the return are nested inside 'if HBmp', so they only run when HBmp is truthy. But the block is only entered when HBmp OR MemDC is falsy. In the case HBmp == 0 (CreateDIBSection failed) and MemDC valid, 'if HBmp' is false, so the entire inner block is skipped: MemDC is never deleted (leak) AND there is no return, so execution falls through to SelectObject(MemDC, HBmp=0), PatBlt, CreatePen, RoundRect and UpdateLayeredWindow with a null bitmap.

allFindings.proposed_fix:
Replace with explicit braces and unconditional cleanup+return:
  if (!HBmp or !MemDC) {
      if (HBmp)
          DllCall("Gdi32\DeleteObject", "Ptr", HBmp)
      if (MemDC)
          DllCall("Gdi32\DeleteDC", "Ptr", MemDC)
      return
  }
so both handles are released regardless of which failed and the function always bails on failure.
